{"name":"Golf Green","hex":"#009b75","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Golf of the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ball game played by individuals competing against one another in which the object is to hit a ball into each of a series of (usually 18 or nine) holes in the minimum number of strokes.","Alternative letter-case form of Golf from the NATO/ICAO Phonetic Alphabet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golf"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golf Green","blocks":[{"text":"Golf Green is a locality of South Kolkata in West Bengal, India. The neighbourhood derives its name from the Royal Calcutta Golf Club, which is located in the vicinity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/View_from_South_City_Mall.jpg/330px-View_from_South_City_Mall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golf_Green","wikidata":"Q5580583"},{"title":"Golf","blocks":[{"text":"Golf is a club-and-ball sport in which players use various clubs to hit a ball into a series of holes on a course in as few strokes as possible. The game was first played in the 15th century in Scotland, and as such, the country is often accredited as \"the home of Golf\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Golfer_swing.jpg/330px-Golfer_swing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golf","wikidata":"Q5377"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ming_Emperor_Xuande_playing_Golf.jpg/500px-Ming_Emperor_Xuande_playing_Golf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ming_Emperor_Xuande_playing_Golf.jpg/500px-Ming_Emperor_Xuande_playing_Golf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Xuande Emperor of the Ming dynasty playing chuiwan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMing_Emperor_Xuande_playing_Golf.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Gloucester%2C_east_window_detail_%2830235780940%29.jpg/500px-Gloucester%2C_east_window_detail